1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,257
TIMELINE is for questions about when.

2
00:00:02,257 --> 00:00:06,771
Several signals are stacked on one scrubbable axis with a
shared playhead,

3
00:00:06,771 --> 00:00:11,895
so you can line up the moment a flag flips against the moment
a value starts moving.

4
00:00:11,895 --> 00:00:16,836
It also syncs to a video of the dashboard or the road, with an
adjustable offset,

5
00:00:16,836 --> 00:00:20,496
so you can match what the bus did to what the car was doing.

6
00:00:20,496 --> 00:00:25,150
Once the definitions are right, CODE GEN turns them into a
working program.

7
00:00:25,150 --> 00:00:28,253
Pick the signals, the interface and the direction,

8
00:00:28,253 --> 00:00:34,024
and it writes Python that opens the bus and decodes those
signals, or encodes and sends them.

9
00:00:34,024 --> 00:00:39,857
The generated encoder goes through cantools and applies the
counter and checksum convention of

10
00:00:39,857 --> 00:00:45,504
whichever vehicle profile you have selected, so the frames it
produces are properly formed.

11
00:00:45,504 --> 00:00:49,704
Your work leaves in whatever format the next tool needs.

12
00:00:49,704 --> 00:00:53,530
A standard DBC that cantools and Vector tools read.

13
00:00:53,530 --> 00:00:58,930
An openpilot DBC with the comma dot ai checksum and counter
annotations.

14
00:00:58,930 --> 00:01:03,956
Vector CANdb++ with the cycle times measured from your own
capture.

15
00:01:03,956 --> 00:01:06,281
AUTOSAR ARXML four point three.

16
00:01:06,281 --> 00:01:12,431
And a Wireshark Lua dissector, so your signals appear by name
in a packet capture.

17
00:01:12,431 --> 00:01:19,407
Each of these is verified in the test suite by loading it back
with the tool that has to read

18
00:01:19,407 --> 00:01:19,632
it.

19
00:01:19,632 --> 00:01:22,366
DIAGNOSTICS is the request side.

20
00:01:22,366 --> 00:01:23,647
UDS, ISO 14229:

21
00:01:23,647 --> 00:01:31,848
read diagnostic trouble codes, read ECU identification, and
scan which services an ECU supports.

22
00:01:31,848 --> 00:01:39,024
That scan is read-only by default, because probing a service
like ECU Reset or Clear

23
00:01:39,024 --> 00:01:43,979
Diagnostics on a live bus does exactly what the name says.

24
00:01:43,979 --> 00:01:51,069
There is OBD-II mode one with automatic PID discovery, J1939,
a bus health monitor,

25
00:01:51,069 --> 00:01:56,280
XCP for reading ECU memory, and DoIP for diagnostics over IP.

26
00:01:56,280 --> 00:02:02,425
Security access, service 0x27, is the seed and key exchange
that unlocks a protected session.

27
00:02:02,425 --> 00:02:08,702
CanLab requests a seed and tries the common algorithms, or
your own key function from a script.

28
00:02:08,702 --> 00:02:12,270
Brute force is available for short keys, rate limited,

29
00:02:12,270 --> 00:02:17,159
and it stops the moment the ECU reports that the attempt limit
is reached,

30
00:02:17,159 --> 00:02:23,040
because tripping that counter can lock a module until it is
power cycled, or permanently.
